# f1 = lesser, f2 = picked. Link says nothing of what `kept` holds: what is stored is a parameter of a join. The literal was born for boxed values, the front of the typed copy asks a list that takes numbers for doubles,
# so the copy that raises the length was never entered, and a fence kept the store as it is written. In lesser() every value that meets in the join is reckoned by the text of the turn (a sum with a number written, a product):
# the literal is born for doubles behind its room, and the typed copy raises its length and stores raw. That the value is a number that is itself is asked in the turn: what is not begins the turn again as it is written.
# In picked() what meets is read out of a list: nothing says that it is a number, and the literal stays as it is written.
# f3 = long, f4 = masked. Where a turn may throw, the literal is given no more room at its birth than the profile of a site may ask (25), and past its room the typed copy is left for good. In long() every turn reads `given` under the
# counter, and a turn of the typed copy that finds no element there leaves the copy: the copy fills no more than `given` is long. Behind the tests of the front the literal is given the lesser of the count and that length: memory that
# stands there already, once more. In masked() no list says how far the typed copy goes: the literal stays as it is written.
# RUN(LESSER): walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# LESSER: NewArray {{*}} arrayStorage=Double
# LESSER: isLoopHead=true
# LESSER: ExtendArrayLength
# LESSER-NEXT: PutDoubleIndex
# RUN(PICKED): walk f2:Body mechanisms=all
# PICKED-NOT: NewArray {{*}} arrayStorage=Double
# PICKED-NOT: ExtendArrayLength
# RUN(LONG): walk f3:Body mechanisms=all
# LONG: NewArray {{*}} arrayStorage=Double
# LONG: [[length]]: {{*}} = GetArrayLength
# LONG: ReserveArrayCapacity
# LONG: isLoopHead=true
# LONG: ExtendArrayLength
# LONG-NEXT: PutDoubleIndex
# RUN(MASKED): walk f4:Body mechanisms=all
# MASKED-NOT: NewArray {{*}} arrayStorage=Double
# MASKED-NOT: ExtendArrayLength
